| MAN  | Metropolitan Area Network | City              | University campuses |
| WAN  | Wide Area Network         | Country / World   | Internet            |

What is Network Topology?

A network topology is the way devices are connected in a network.

Think of it as the layout or design of the network.

1. Star Topology 

Every device connects to a central switch or router.

        Switch
      /   |   \
    PC  Laptop Printer
Advantages
Easy to manage
Easy to add new devices
One device failure doesn't affect others
Disadvantage

If the central switch fails,
the whole network stops working.

Used In
Offices
Schools
Companies

2. Bus Topology

All devices share one main cable.

PC ----- PC ----- PC ----- PC
Advantages
Cheap
Easy to set up
Disadvantages
Main cable failure affects everyone
Difficult to troubleshoot

Used very rarely today.

3. Ring Topology

Devices form a circle.

PC ---- PC
|        |
PC ---- PC

Data travels around the ring.

Advantage
Organized communication
Disadvantage

If one connection breaks,
the whole ring can be affected.

4. Mesh Topology

Every device connects to every other device.

A ------- B
|\       /|
| \     / |
|  \   /  |
|   \ /   |
D ------- C
Advantages
Very reliable
Multiple paths for communication
Disadvantages
Expensive
Complex

Used in:

Military
Data Centers
Critical infrastructure

5. Tree Topology

Looks like a tree structure.

          Core Switch
          /        \
     Switch      Switch
      /   \        |
    PC   PC      Laptop

Common in:

Schools
Companies
Large organizations

6. Hybrid Topology

A combination of two or more topologies.

Example:

A company may use:

Star topology in offices
Mesh topology between data centers

Most modern organizations use a hybrid topology.
